{"questions": [{"header":"Rasterization approach","message":"**Client-side (in-browser)**: When the author picks an icon + color in the picker dialog, JS in the canvas-theme-editor app converts that one SVG to a PNG on the fly (e.g. draw the SVG into an off-screen `<canvas>` via an `Image`/data-URI, then `canvas.toBlob()`). No server or build step involved — works for any icon/color combo instantly, but the PNG only exists as a downloadable/copyable blob in that user's session; nothing is hosted at a stable URL unless you separately upload it. Simple to build, but 'host on CDN for RCE copy-paste' isn't automatic — you'd need a manual upload step (e.g. to Canvas's own file storage) since we chose no new hosting infra.

**Build-time (like docs/scripts/gen-og.ts)**: A script (using `@resvg/resvg-js`, which needs Node, not the browser) runs during the repo's build/release, pre-rendering every icon × color (13 + black/white = 15) combination into real PNG files, which get published as static assets in a package (e.g. `@pantoken/icon-font` or a new `@pantoken/icons-png` format) and thus become fetchable via the existing `@pantoken/cdn` (jsDelivr/unpkg) — satisfying 'hosted somewhere to be importable via copy/paste' directly, matching your CDN-hosting answer. Tradeoff: build step complexity, larger published package size (icon count × 15 colors), and any new/changed icon needs a rebuild.

Given you chose CDN hosting for import, build-time generation is the natural fit — recommending that.","options":[{"label":"Build-time script, published to CDN (matches your hosting answer)","recommended":true},{"label":"Client-side rasterization only, add hosting/upload separately"}],"question":"Given you want CDN-hosted PNGs for RCE import, should rasterization happen at build time (pre-generate all icon×color PNGs, publish via CDN) or client-side (rasterize on demand, then handle upload separately)?"}]}

